# Windows launchers must keep CRLF line endings.
#
# cmd.exe is unreliable with LF-only .bat files: a multi-line `if` block or a
# trailing `pause` can be mis-parsed, which would strand an operator whose only
# entry point is a double-click. PowerShell is more tolerant, but the two
# launchers are maintained as a pair, so both are pinned.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Everything else keeps LF in the repository.
*.py  text eol=lf
*.sh  text eol=lf
*.md  text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# Byte-hashed evidence files must have canonical bytes on every host.
#
# Two files are pinned by sha256 over raw bytes -- config/lane_a_freeze.sha256
# and requirements.txt -- by AHOS_LAPTOP_OPERATION_REPORT.md /
# reports/local_laptop_baseline.json and by
# tests/test_phase13_laptop_operation.py::test_operation_report_dependency_hashes_are_real.
# A checkout is free to rewrite the line endings of any path with no rule here
# (core.autocrlf does exactly that on Windows), which makes such a byte hash a
# property of the host rather than the repository: one commit then records a
# different hash on Linux and Windows. That is how M-GAP-042 presented --
# lane_a_freeze.sha256 is committed with LF but was checked out with CRLF on
# this Windows host, so its working-tree sha256 (8d35cd2b...) stopped matching
# the documented one (2f5d67dd...).
#
# -text pins the working-tree bytes to the committed blob in BOTH directions:
# no conversion on check-out and none on check-in, so sha256(read_bytes()) is
# a property of the repository on any host. text eol=lf|crlf was rejected for
# these two: eol= only governs check-out, and git still normalises on check-in
# (verified -- staging requirements.txt under `text eol=crlf` rewrote its blob
# from CRLF to LF, which would have silently changed its documented hash).
# Neither file's committed content changes here.
*.sha256         -text
requirements.txt -text
